import json
from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]; data=json.loads((R/'data/americas.json').read_text()); c=json.loads((R/'research/americas-coverage.json').read_text());new=[];ratings={};notes={}
def a(y,n,d,t,s,st,why,sol=None,note=None):
 ratings.setdefault(y,{})[str(n)]=d;notes.setdefault(y,{})[str(n)]=why
 if not 4<=d<=7:return
 u=f'https://cms.math.ca/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/sol{y}.pdf'
 new.append(dict(id=f'ca-cmo-{y}-{n}',country='Canada',competition='CMO',year=y,section='National final',number=str(n),statement=st,solution=sol or f'[Read the official worked solution, Problem {n}]({u}).',sourceUrl=f'https://cms.math.ca/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/exam{y}.pdf',solutionUrl=u,solutionKind='original-exposition' if sol else 'source-link',topic=t,tags=s,subtopics=s,difficulty=d,ratingReason=why,reviewStatus='individually-assessed',notes=note or 'Statement restated in original wording. Official CMS worked solution reviewed.'))
a(2009,1,4,'Combinatorics',['Counting','Recurrences & generating functions'],r'Colour the cells of a $2\times n$ board black or white. A black cell is forbidden if both a white cell somewhere to its left in the same row and a white cell somewhere above it in the same column exist. Find a closed formula for the number of permitted colourings.','Condition on the position of the first white cell in the lower row. Before it columns have two choices, afterwards three; evaluating the resulting geometric sum gives 2·3ⁿ−2ⁿ.')
a(2009,2,3.5,'Combinatorics',[],None,'Across all 200 rotations, each sector agrees with exactly 100 opposite sectors; the average agreement count is 100.')
a(2009,3,3.5,'Algebra',[],None,'Rewrite as 1+xyz/((x+y)(y+z)(z+x)); three AM–GM bounds give the upper endpoint, and setting y=z gives all intermediate values by continuity.')
a(2009,4,6.5,'Number Theory',['Diophantine equations','Modular arithmetic','Divisibility & GCD'],r'Find all integer pairs $(a,b)$ for which $3^a+7^b$ is the square of an integer.','Modulo 4 forces opposite exponent parity. Factoring a difference of squares reduces each case to a prime-power difference; the harder branch needs divisibility of b by 3 and a second factorization to exclude larger exponents.',sol=r'''Negative exponents cannot occur: if one exponent is negative, clearing the powers of 3 and 7 in the denominators leaves a numerator coprime to a nontrivial denominator. Thus $a,b\ge0$. Write $3^a+7^b=N^2$. Modulo 4, exactly one of $a,b$ is odd.

If $a$ is odd and $b=2c$, then $(N-7^c)(N+7^c)=3^a$. The factors cannot both be divisible by 3, so $N-7^c=1$ and $3^a=2\cdot7^c+1$. For $c=0$ this gives $(a,b)=(1,0)$. For $c>0$, reduction modulo 7 would force $6\mid a$, impossible.

If $a=2c$ and $b$ is odd, similarly $N-3^c=1$ and $7^b=2\cdot3^c+1$. The case $c=0$ is impossible; $c=1$ gives $(a,b)=(2,1)$. For $c\ge2$, modulo 9 gives $3\mid b$. Put $b=3d$ and $Y=7^d$. Then $(Y-1)(Y^2+Y+1)=2\cdot3^c$. Since $Y\equiv1\pmod3$, the second factor has exactly one factor of 3: writing $Y=1+3h$ gives $Y^2+Y+1=3(1+3h+3h^2)$. It has no other prime factors and is odd, so it would equal 3, contradicting $Y\ge7$. The two listed pairs both work.''')
a(2009,5,7,'Geometry',['Combinatorial geometry','Extremal arguments','Circles & tangency'],r'Let $S$ be a set of at least three points in the plane. Suppose every three distinct points of $S$ lie in some closed disk of radius 1. Prove that one closed disk of radius 1 contains all of $S$.','The smallest enclosing circle is controlled by two antipodal boundary points or by three boundary points surrounding its centre. The unrestricted infinite-set statement additionally needs a compactness step beyond the official finite-set proof.',sol=r'''First let $S$ be finite. Its smallest enclosing disk exists. Its boundary points cannot all lie in an open semicircle: moving the centre slightly towards that semicircle would strictly decrease all maximal distances, while the remaining finitely many points remain strictly inside. If there are two antipodal boundary points, every disk containing them has radius at least that of the minimal disk. Otherwise choose three boundary points not lying in an open semicircle. Such a triple exists by ordering boundary points around the circle: its centre belongs to the convex hull of the boundary points, and in the plane a convex-hull point belongs to a triangle of vertices. For completeness, a polygon can be triangulated from one vertex, giving that last fact.

Write the centre as $O=\sum_{i=1}^3\lambda_i A_i$, where $\lambda_i\ge0$ and $\sum\lambda_i=1$. If the minimal radius is $r$, then for any $Z$,
$$\sum\lambda_i|A_i-Z|^2=r^2+|O-Z|^2\ge r^2.$$
Thus no disk containing this triple has radius less than $r$. The hypothesis gives $r\le1$. The antipodal case follows from the hypothesis by adjoining any third point.

For an infinite $S$, fix $q\in S$. For each $p\in S$, possible covering centres form the closed disk $D(p,1)$. Intersect these with the compact disk $D(q,1)$. Every finite subfamily has nonempty intersection by the finite result (adjoin extra points if it has fewer than three). The finite-intersection property of compact sets now gives a centre common to all these disks.''',note='The printed question omits a cardinality qualification and the official solution addresses only finite sets. The wording explicitly assumes at least three points to avoid vacuous two-point counterexamples; the original exposition supplies the infinite-set extension.')
a(2008,1,4.5,'Geometry',['Area & dissection','Similarity & congruence'],r'In convex quadrilateral $ABCD$, side $AB$ is the longest. Points $M\in AB$ and $N\in BC$ are chosen so that each of $CM$ and $AN$ divides the quadrilateral into equal areas. Prove that $MN$ passes through the midpoint of $BD$.','Equal areas first force MN parallel AC. Extend BA and BC to the parallel through D; a second area comparison makes M,N the midpoints of the enlarged triangle sides.')
a(2008,2,4.5,'Algebra',['Functional equations'],r'Find all functions $f:\mathbb Q\to\mathbb Q$ such that $f(2f(x)+f(y))=2x+y$ for all rational $x,y$.','The diagonal substitution forces f(9x)=9f(x), hence f(0)=0. Then f is an involution and the equation becomes additivity; rationality reduces it to f(x)=±x.')
a(2008,3,4.5,'Algebra',['Inequalities','Algebraic identities'],r'If positive $a,b,c$ satisfy $a+b+c=1$, prove $\frac{a-bc}{a+bc}+\frac{b-ca}{b+ca}+\frac{c-ab}{c+ab}\le\frac32$.','Use a+bc=(a+b)(a+c), then combine the fractions into 1+4abc/((a+b)(b+c)(c+a)). The final bound is AM–GM, but the common-denominator identity is the decisive step.')
a(2008,4,6.5,'Number Theory',['Functional equations','Primes','Modular arithmetic'],r'Classify functions $f:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$, with $\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, for which $f(n)^p\equiv n\pmod{f(p)}$ for every positive integer $n$ and prime $p$.','Prime inputs force f(p) to be either 1 or p. Infinitely many fixed primes force the identity by Fermat; finitely many require a Euclidean construction excluding any largest odd fixed prime. The surviving parity family must then be checked.')
a(2008,5,6.5,'Combinatorics',['Recurrences & generating functions','Combinatorial geometry'],r'A path on a $3\times n$ grid of cells starts in the lower-left cell, ends in the upper-left cell, moves between side-adjacent cells, and visits no cell twice. Paths are distinguished by their visited sequence of cells. Determine their number as a closed formula in $n$.','Classifying paths by their use of the leftmost middle cell yields rₙ₊₁=2+2rₙ+rₙ₋₁. Proving the classification is exhaustive is the main work; shifting rₙ by 1 then solves the recurrence.',note='The source calls these self-avoiding rook walks and counts geometric unit-cell paths, not arbitrary subdivisions of one straight rook move. This wording makes that convention explicit.')
a(2007,1,5,'Combinatorics',['Colouring','Tilings & dissections'],r'Number the rows of an $8\times9$ board from top to bottom and columns from left to right. Six fixed horizontal dominoes occupy cells $(r,9-r)$ and $(r,10-r)$ for $r=2,3,\ldots,7$. What is the greatest total number of nonoverlapping dominoes that can cover pairs of side-adjacent cells, including these six?','The fixed diagonal barrier splits the free board into two regions with opposite checkerboard imbalances. Adding the two corner cells still permits only 14 additional dominoes per side; a matching construction reaches 34.',note='The six fixed dominoes were transcribed as exact cell coordinates after visually checking the official diagram.')
a(2007,2,4.5,'Geometry',['Similarity & congruence','Geometric inequalities'],r'Two similar triangles have two side lengths in common. Show that the similarity ratio lies strictly between $(\sqrt5-1)/2$ and $(\sqrt5+1)/2$.','If the triangles are not congruent, ordering their three side lengths forces the overlapping lengths to form a geometric progression. The triangle inequality becomes r²<r+1; the reciprocal gives the other endpoint.',note='The official paper prints the lower endpoint twice. The corrected upper endpoint follows from the official solution and is stated explicitly here.')
a(2007,3,5,'Algebra',['Functional equations','Inequalities'],r'A function $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ obeys $f(xy)+f(y-x)\ge f(y+x)$ for all real $x,y$. Give a nonconstant polynomial example and prove that $f(t)\ge0$ for every real $t$.','Choose xy=x+y so two function terms cancel, and show y−x covers every real number under this constraint. The polynomial example x²+4 emerges by completing a square.')
a(2007,4,5,'Algebra',['Algebraic identities','Functions & graphs'],r'For $ab\ne1$ put $a*b=(a+b-2ab)/(1-ab)$. Starting from $n\ge2$ numbers in $(0,1)$, repeatedly replace any pair by their $*$-value. Prove that the final number is independent of all choices. What happens if the initial numbers lie in $(0,1]$ with exactly one equal to 1?','The substitution φ(x)=x/(1−x) turns the operation into addition. A complete proof also verifies closure of (0,1); the endpoint case follows because 1 is absorbing.')
a(2007,5,7,'Geometry',['Circles & tangency','Similarity & congruence','Concurrency & collinearity'],r'The incircle of $ABC$ touches $BC,CA,AB$ at $D,E,F$. The circumcircle of $ABC$ meets $(AEF),(BDF),(CDE)$ again at $P,Q,R$, respectively. Prove that the latter three circles have a common point, and that $PD,QE,RF$ concur.','The common point is the incentre. For concurrency, linked cyclic-angle similarities identify the lines with joins of contact points to circumcircle arc midpoints; a homothety then gives concurrence. This requires a sustained auxiliary-circle argument.')
a(2006,1,5.5,'Combinatorics',['Counting','Recurrences & generating functions'],r'Let $f(n,k)$ count distributions of $k$ identical candies among $n$ distinguishable children with at most two candies per child. Evaluate $f(2006,1)+f(2006,4)+\cdots+f(2006,1003)$.','The printed endpoint prevents the intended simple roots-of-unity count. Removing one child turns the sum into a partial coefficient sum of (1+x+x²)^2005, and inclusion–exclusion gives an exact finite binomial expression.',sol=r'''Condition on the last child’s 0, 1, or 2 candies. Summing the three resulting terms for each total $1,4,\ldots,1003$ covers every remaining total from 0 to 1003 exactly once. Thus the answer is $\sum_{k=0}^{1003}[x^k](1+x+x^2)^{2005}$, equivalently
$$[x^{1003}]\frac{(1-x^3)^{2005}}{(1-x)^{2006}}
=\sum_{j=0}^{334}(-1)^j\binom{2005}{j}\binom{3008-3j}{2005}.$$
This exact integer expression answers the paper as printed. The organizer notes that the intended upper endpoint was 4012, which would instead give $3^{2005}$; that is a different sum.''',note='The official solution identifies a contest printing error: intended endpoint 4012, actual endpoint 1003. The published statement here preserves 1003. The binomial sum starts at j=0, correcting a dropped initial term in one displayed official formula.')
a(2006,2,3.5,'Geometry',[],None,'The midpoint of the rectangle’s upper edge moves along the A-median; halving its altitude gives a segment joining the midpoint of BC to the midpoint of the altitude.')
a(2006,3,5,'Combinatorics',['Double counting'],r'An $m\times n$ array of nonnegative reals has a positive entry in every row and column. Whenever an entry is positive, its row sum equals its column sum. Prove that $m=n$.','Normalize each positive entry by its row sum, which equals its column sum. The resulting array has every row and column sum equal to 1, so double counting its total gives the result.')
a(2006,4,5.5,'Combinatorics',['Graph theory','Double counting','Extremal arguments'],r'In a round-robin tournament on $2n+1$ teams, every pair plays once and there are no ties. A triple is cyclic if its teams beat one another cyclically. Determine the smallest and largest possible numbers of cyclic triples.','Count noncyclic triples by their unique team with two wins, then minimize a sum of squared outdegrees. A regular cyclic tournament attains the upper bound; a transitive tournament gives zero.')
a(2006,5,6,'Geometry',['Circles & tangency','Angles & cyclic quadrilaterals'],r'A right triangle $ABC$, with right angle at $A$, is inscribed in a circle. On each of the three arcs $BC,CA,AB$ choose a point $D,E,F$, respectively, whose tangent meets lines $AB,AC$ at endpoints with the point of tangency as their midpoint. Prove $DEF$ equilateral.','The midpoint-of-hypotenuse property converts the tangent condition into equal distances from A. Tangent-chord angle relations then show each relevant arc is one third of the circumference.')
a(2005,1,4,'Combinatorics',['Counting','Combinatorial geometry'],r'An equilateral triangle of side $n$ is subdivided by the standard triangular grid into unit equilateral triangles. Count paths from the unique top-row cell to the middle bottom-row cell that cross shared edges, never move to a higher row, and never revisit a cell. Evaluate the count for $n=2005$.','The path is determined uniquely by its crossing edge between each pair of consecutive rows. There are 1,2,…,n−1 independent choices; recognizing that independence gives (n−1)!.')
a(2005,2,4.5,'Number Theory',['Diophantine equations','Divisibility & GCD'],r'If positive integers $a,b,c$ satisfy $a^2+b^2=c^2$, prove that $(c/a+c/b)^2>8$ and that this quantity is never an integer.','AM–GM gives the bound, with equality excluded by irrationality of √2. For integrality, reduce to a primitive triple and use that a rational square root of an integer is integral, then parity or coprimality gives a contradiction.')
a(2005,3,6,'Geometry',['Combinatorial geometry','Extremal arguments'],r'Let $S$ consist of $n\ge3$ points strictly inside a circle. Prove that three distinct points of $S$ can each be the unique nearest point of $S$ to a respective point on the circle. Show by examples that four such points cannot be guaranteed, however large $n$ is.','Supporting lines at three convex-hull boundary points give the nearest-point witnesses, including a separate collinear case. The sharp counterexample uses three side midpoints of an equilateral triangle plus a central cluster.')
a(2005,4,4.5,'Geometry',['Geometric inequalities','Trigonometry'],r'For a triangle of area $K$, perimeter $P$, and circumradius $R$, determine the greatest possible value of $KP/R^3$.','The sine rule rewrites the ratio as 4 sinA sinB sinC(sinA+sinB+sinC). AM–GM followed by concavity of sine makes the equilateral equality case sharp.')
a(2005,5,6.5,'Number Theory',['Sequences & recurrences','Modular arithmetic','Primes'],r'Call a triple of positive integers $a\le b\le c$ with $\gcd(a,b,c)=1$ $n$-powerful when $a+b+c\mid a^n+b^n+c^n$. Find the triples powerful for every $n\ge1$. Also find those powerful for $n=2004,2005$ but not for $n=2007$.','A cubic-root recurrence links power sums three steps apart and immediately resolves the second part. Fermat and residues modulo 4 and 9 restrict the sum to 3 or 6, leaving a short enumeration for the first part.')
a(2004,1,3,'Algebra',[],None,'Substituting a=x+1,b=y+1,c=z+1 gives ab=c,ac=b,bc=a; the zero and nonzero cases yield five triples.')
a(2004,2,4.5,'Combinatorics',['Counting','Permutations & arrangements'],r'How many placements of eight indistinguishable rooks on a standard $9\times9$ checkerboard have no two in a common row or column and put all rooks on cells of one colour?','Split rows and columns by parity. One colour decomposes into 5×5 and 4×4 blocks, the other into two 5×4 blocks; tracking the empty row gives 14·5!·4!.')
a(2004,3,5.5,'Geometry',['Angles & cyclic quadrilaterals','Circles & tangency'],r'Points $A,B,C,D$ occur in this order on a circle, with $AB<AD$ and $BC>CD$. The internal bisectors of $\angle BAD$ and $\angle BCD$ meet the circle again at $X,Y$. If four sides of the resulting six-vertex cyclic polygon are equal, prove that $BD$ is a diameter.','The strict chord inequalities determine the order of X,Y and eliminate the two longer sides from the equal group. The remaining four equal arcs force both half-angles to be 45 degrees.')
a(2004,4,5,'Number Theory',['Modular arithmetic','Factorials & binomial coefficients'],r'For every odd prime $p$, prove $\sum_{k=1}^{p-1}k^{2p-1}\equiv p(p+1)/2\pmod{p^2}$.','Pair k and p−k, retain only the two surviving binomial terms modulo p², and use Fermat modulo p on the remaining even exponent. The modulus-squared bookkeeping is the main step.')
a(2004,5,5,'Number Theory',['Divisibility & GCD','Pigeonhole principle'],r'Among the positive divisors of $2004^{100}$, how large can a subset be if no two distinct chosen elements divide one another?','Prime-exponent coordinates give an upper bound by fixing the exponents of 3 and 167. The matching constant-total-exponent layer is possible because the exponent range for 2 is twice as long.')
ids={r['id'] for r in new};data=[r for r in data if r['id'] not in ids]+new
(R/'data/americas.json').write_text(json.dumps(data,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
for y,rr in ratings.items():
 c['fullyScreened']=[p for p in c['fullyScreened'] if not(p['competition']=='CMO' and p['year']==y)]
 c['fullyScreened'].append(dict(competition='CMO',year=y,ratings=rr,source=f'https://cms.math.ca/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/sol{y}.pdf',assessmentNotes=notes[y]))
c['papersScreened']=len(c['fullyScreened']);c['problemAppearancesScreened']=sum(len(p['ratings']) for p in c['fullyScreened']+c['partiallyScreened']);c['uniqueIncluded']=len(data)
c['gaps'][0]='Canada: CMO 2004–2026 and all CJMO 2020–2026 fully screened. CMO 1969–2003 remains under review; official solutions exist from 1994.'
c['verificationNotes'].append('CMO 2004–2009 individually reviewed in full. Official CMO 2007 Q2 prints the lower similarity bound twice; corrected upper endpoint is supported by its solution. CMO 2006 Q1 preserves the actual 1003 endpoint and supplies a corrected finite sum (j=0, not j=1); organizer intended endpoint 4012 is documented separately. CMO 2009 Q4 original proof repairs the source’s unjustified valuation step, and Q5 supplies the missing infinite-set argument with an explicit at-least-three-points qualification.')
(R/'research/americas-coverage.json').write_text(json.dumps(c,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n');print(len(new),'new;',len(data),'total;',c['papersScreened'],'complete papers')
